    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nThe Neovim LSP client writes all logs to a single file (`vim.lsp.get_log_path()`), and the file keeps growing indefinitely.\nLog file is:\n- Append-only\n- Unbounded in size\n\n There is no built-in mechanism for:\n- Rotating logs\n- Limiting file size\n- Trimming old logs\n\nThe default behaviour does not follow the **principle of least surprise** with is an issue especially with novice users.\nThe log file can grow very large with time and require explicit handling from the user.\n\nThis is an issue notably when:\n- Many LSP servers tend to span WARN and sometime ERROR logs.\n- Running Neovim for long periods of time.\n- Working with multiple language servers\n\nEven with logging completely turned OFF (default is WARN), there is always a one line log: \n`[START][2026-06-27 18:22:05] LSP logging initiated`\n\nFor example, with a default neovim install, and clangd used with a large project.\nLsp log can exceed 1GB in just few days.\n\n\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nIt would be useful to have log rotation mechanism  (enabled by default) built-in and used by the LSP logger.\nThis mechanism can be shared with other neovim logs eventually.\n\n## Possible approaches:\n\n### Size-based rotation\n\nAllow configuration of a maximum log size, after which the file is rotated.\n\n### Retention-based rotation\n\nKeep a fixed number of log files, for example:\n\n- `lsp.log`\n- `lsp.log.1`\n- `lsp.log.2`\n- ...\n\n",
    "closed_at": null,
